#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/id #uuid "8cd94856-fbe5-53a4-b7f2-f5b29b7aa927" :question/math-academy-id "e-4514" :question/problem "Find the inverse of $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/id #uuid "9180ff3b-46d2-5df7-81b6-ac75bef51e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-4515" :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "26e9a55f-7711-5d8f-ab0a-e8638f2d2dec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4516" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb72e652-2763-5ceb-bd0f-6c7a5e466cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277143} {:answer/id #uuid "8653a3ef-39f9-51eb-82b1-62da85d47edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277144} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8799de-21d7-5edd-9209-467cd8efb73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277145} {:answer/id #uuid "c1212344-18db-586d-8738-9b91209147ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277146} {:answer/id #uuid "1e68694e-64b0-5d69-84a4-34468affad76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1212344-18db-586d-8738-9b91209147ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277146} :answer-field/id #uuid "3becfc88-cffb-5601-98b6-f559acc656b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb4bf485-78fa-5095-a727-46d78e9ce455" :question/math-academy-id "q-104134" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb32a0e2-596e-56fb-9d4f-0c3e5fff002c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277150} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1e3445-d6c3-54e9-a82e-ff7582e72301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277151} {:answer/id #uuid "95b50e00-c704-585f-af9d-5aca469f47d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277152} {:answer/id #uuid "9671fa34-4879-5b92-8fce-fface7fee115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277153} {:answer/id #uuid "703fc5e1-5a04-5cdf-9dce-ae52eeab7d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff1e3445-d6c3-54e9-a82e-ff7582e72301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277151} :answer-field/id #uuid "323ac2c4-2a39-5cfb-963c-32d6c3c9a442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81c8171-57ec-50af-9be0-d2782830120d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104428" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=2k+2.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2k+2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2k+2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k+1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k+1 & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c86ad2f4-4a1f-586a-b03e-f153ac5dd6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277157} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a55970-1da2-5bbf-8715-0bc932824faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277158} {:answer/id #uuid "4398c1e3-36be-5563-bd51-26cff3e925f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277159} {:answer/id #uuid "6d58c173-a2fb-53d2-b800-4ccd33e19c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277160} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1e7ed2-279d-5b95-86d6-5bd66305fce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4398c1e3-36be-5563-bd51-26cff3e925f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277159} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ea46fb-df49-5a7a-be0a-d7e6dfa7069a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67c2a765-d4f8-5e5e-81ad-52ebfa9d366d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18283" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $-4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =15-(-1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 16=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc2eaecb-9e0a-54c5-a61e-66dfe5bfa99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277164} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6372fb-f32f-5978-bd94-a3e204be63a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277165} {:answer/id #uuid "9b83cfcb-0128-5d79-b6ef-3aec88ec01eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277166} {:answer/id #uuid "b004a483-509e-5f7e-b850-49deaa40ba76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277167} {:answer/id #uuid "9e389c25-4fd3-5d66-8445-d944f69b0c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc2eaecb-9e0a-54c5-a61e-66dfe5bfa99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277164} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e011d3-385d-567e-8459-e7e986ccbfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99ca9db9-93d5-5a9d-9021-745df58ebdc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-18286" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $39.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}=\\frac{1}{39}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{39}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e546d6d3-0978-54ea-bd1c-8e77c8bb8a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277171} {:answer/id #uuid "07a0bca4-9872-5634-9895-3b35e4ee320b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277172} {:answer/id #uuid "d90bf246-234f-5eae-a0e2-7b9c68114c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277173} {:answer/id #uuid "24c35886-1555-5e0e-9203-46617d69f802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277174} {:answer/id #uuid "4363b492-ca9e-5130-8408-aa4e22cda897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e546d6d3-0978-54ea-bd1c-8e77c8bb8a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277171} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c66934-93ee-5f6a-b0a2-d1d2c4a23fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c50173d-87e8-5fa9-94f9-84896e240170" :question/math-academy-id "q-18288" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ if its determinant is $71.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-3-10) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{71}\\cdot 13=\\frac{13}{71}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f22e8b-8172-5755-9788-5152ad8d0d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277178} {:answer/id #uuid "a64f6ef3-ada2-526e-9a4e-b0165f510a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a64f6ef3-ada2-526e-9a4e-b0165f510a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277179} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4dcadb5-0e0c-5dd6-9d22-47d3c0740250" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76a33450-1f1e-5ba4-9578-1f22a522bff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-275756" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=-k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-k)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22354b23-f75c-50c2-80df-844f85a7f423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22354b23-f75c-50c2-80df-844f85a7f423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277182} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d42a07-0606-5394-ac28-4d1a35d2a48b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e301e6b9-3976-597e-9fb0-a8b45765d11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e301e6b9-3976-597e-9fb0-a8b45765d11c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277184} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba4f7bc-587a-51c8-87e6-378d764722ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcc61cd-f851-511c-84a7-6d09f7a65d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fcc61cd-f851-511c-84a7-6d09f7a65d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277186} :answer-field/id #uuid "2226d888-1340-59d6-8cbb-eeab11e30423" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e3fe030-755d-5041-98d0-9545fc141827" :question/math-academy-id "q-319370" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | $4$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $7$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4cd7880-cbeb-5e17-a522-cdef933ac288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189} {:answer/id #uuid "d1569719-0ed3-55b5-8f03-c3c74fab988d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd7880-cbeb-5e17-a522-cdef933ac288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e026ef3-c31f-5ebc-ae8a-d3f8569779a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277188} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56561db0-49a6-50f2-a2c2-c92dfd5c3a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56561db0-49a6-50f2-a2c2-c92dfd5c3a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277192} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f591c37-0c4a-500d-9205-616cd965bdde" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd1c628-5e96-5d0f-9bc9-49d3b7d65f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd1c628-5e96-5d0f-9bc9-49d3b7d65f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277194} :answer-field/id #uuid "4626966b-9bc6-5924-afd8-53449186a7f4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8122a778-d775-58c3-a04a-f92b984b0b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8122a778-d775-58c3-a04a-f92b984b0b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277196} :answer-field/id #uuid "f655b395-ec65-58be-aad8-7318e72596a9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29d21586-1288-572a-99e4-232cb8821d62" :question/math-academy-id "q-319395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | $-1$ | $-3$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ {{field-2}} |  | $-1$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d227f88a-1d1b-5106-92c1-bfd6998feac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d227f88a-1d1b-5106-92c1-bfd6998feac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199} :answer-field/id #uuid "1808c969-ad9d-51b2-9fb1-aa521f99ebb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67ba84de-9203-5dc9-b081-a5adbd6f7175" :question/math-academy-id "q-319416" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2-(-3))+2(4-0)+0(6-0) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1+4) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e12872-6819-5edf-b754-61f302f06a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14e12872-6819-5edf-b754-61f302f06a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277202} :answer-field/id #uuid "94898792-e273-59f3-abe1-121d9c5b47f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0638d225-13ff-517f-a049-cbd1724d2bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319549" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\,(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e88939-b056-55d9-815f-f23dfbbd0dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90e88939-b056-55d9-815f-f23dfbbd0dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277205} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e22d16-3898-5e24-b26e-2a73034a64ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64de48f6-7f97-5a35-baf2-6da36ea66dca" :question/math-academy-id "q-319944" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148a36ce-70ef-5668-b6e8-fbf694e2600e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 18 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277208} {:answer/id #uuid "8dad0546-0c0f-5836-afb9-77ece8d4330d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -18 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277209} {:answer/id #uuid "1278584e-88c5-55fd-81cf-179b62403056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277210} {:answer/id #uuid "57f646ce-3f7b-5385-92b2-c15f53f31c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277211} {:answer/id #uuid "36dfbf46-be46-5e70-9b7a-6bdd6273122e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36dfbf46-be46-5e70-9b7a-6bdd6273122e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277212} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb691e-d16b-5700-a5ff-50fa4e7c6dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d1042ad-eb6e-59f8-9dab-abab5265f662" :question/math-academy-id "q-73485" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\ngiven that $det(A)=12.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -18 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 18 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624ed0b2-ed83-5a54-9bac-a397b45be3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277215} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27249c-e976-5a92-a381-bd76d21e2160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277216} {:answer/id #uuid "49409bb8-a89f-5d4e-87b1-0f2174cae34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277217} {:answer/id #uuid "244176fc-ac03-592b-b02d-dd85dc206bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277218} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1a004d-11df-5099-8994-ea5250142975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "624ed0b2-ed83-5a54-9bac-a397b45be3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277215} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f27ca11-09b4-5703-89d3-94d3cd2cbec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00d9a881-5bc6-5ed9-85ff-a17a1eb00b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-73535" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=6+3k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=6+3k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2-(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6+3k}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2+k} \\\\ 2+k & =4 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd046539-25a7-564a-91bb-b2394efa19e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277222} {:answer/id #uuid "e02d9026-e0ad-5b31-bbc2-110ef84446d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277223} {:answer/id #uuid "1eece0a9-bb6a-5d14-9308-d4320a6301a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277224} {:answer/id #uuid "c84d3b4a-2d3f-597a-be4b-50fc0db8626c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277225} {:answer/id #uuid "18d11d93-13f4-548f-8637-a9da8f8f4319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18d11d93-13f4-548f-8637-a9da8f8f4319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277226} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a03d3b-4013-5029-901d-a8665f2c094b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af367a5a-98da-5413-9bce-53cbcdc43cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-73545" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{4}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-k-6)-(0-3) \\\\  & =-2k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{11}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{11} & =(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-9}\\cdot (-k-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+6}{2k+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{11}^{′}=\\frac{4}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+6}{2k+9} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 5(k+6) & =4(2k+9) \\\\ 5k+30 & =8k+36 \\\\ 3k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51453a18-f217-50a1-bb63-13490f799f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277229} {:answer/id #uuid "11bd6433-215e-544c-a4e8-df83e70cad38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 9 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277230} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ff5db1-40da-5f98-a90c-e50ddf7ff9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -10 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277231} {:answer/id #uuid "cc801eb8-0369-523b-a994-ebe95105fed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277232} {:answer/id #uuid "139ba8ce-9b61-5c85-9a04-3655e842306b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "139ba8ce-9b61-5c85-9a04-3655e842306b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277233} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd42d47-a772-50ff-89e8-99a1278df0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8e0bc68-4b39-55ac-8170-1361143f9b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9967" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =4|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
